<CaseStudy
  title="Early years, 2003 to 2013"
  description="How a Call of Duty clan writer ended up at FACEIT."
  image="tl-02-call-of-duty"
  meta="2003–2013 · 4Kings, Stermy.com, FPS Omega"
  next={{ href: '/work/fnatic', title: 'Fnatic, 2008 to 2011' }}
>
  <p>I loved the slow, methodical play of Call of Duty 1 and 2. I mostly played rifle, sometimes shotgun. Then Call of Duty 4 took that away completely: fast pace, cooking nades, sprinting, SMGs everywhere. I didn't like it, but I wanted to stay involved. So I started writing about my own team, managing them, scheduling matches and trying to find sponsors. The writing took off, and soon I was writing about other teams too.</p>

  <h2>4Kings (2007 to 2008)</h2>
  <p>My first proper role was as an editor covering Call of Duty for 4Kings, one of the UK's oldest esports organisations. I had a lot of freedom, and I loved the org and the team, even if the players weren't that engaged with the content. At Fnatic the players were much more on board, and I still speak to some of the Swedes now and then.</p>

  <h2>Stermy (2010)</h2>
  <p>Alessandro 'Stermy' Avallone played Quake in Fnatic's 4v4 team, but he was kind of freelance: in duel he played under his own brand. We got on well, so I asked if he needed help writing for his own website. He did, and that's how our friendship started. It was my first close look at how a player's own brand turns into a community.</p>

  <h2>FPS Omega (2011 to 2013)</h2>
  <p>I started FPS Omega to write on my own platform. Its main site, ShootManiaEsports.com, became the leading English-language site for ShootMania, a growing game with a lot of backing from Ubisoft. We had a few thousand impressions a day, which was fairly sizable for esports at the time. We also started an Overwatch portal, but I never had the time to grow it. When FACEIT took over my time I had no one to hand FPS Omega to, so it stopped.</p>

  <h2>How it led to FACEIT</h2>
  <p>Stermy was playing ShootMania by then, and he was probably the biggest star in the game. I interviewed him a lot, because he kept winning our MVP awards. At some point he asked if I'd be interested in talking to his co-founders about a project he was working on. He introduced me to Michele, and after one call I flew to Milan to meet Michele and Niccolò. I decided to join FACEIT there and then. I had wild long locks back then.</p>
</CaseStudy>
